        'en' => [
            'title' => 'Before the blood draw, show the hair supplement too',
            'image_alt' => 'An unmarked teal supplement bottle beside a blood tube with a purple stopper and a magnifying glass on pale paper. Purple and teal tones, clean illustration without text.',
            'excerpt' => 'High doses of biotin in hair and nail supplements can distort some laboratory results. In kidney disease this also matters for parathyroid hormone and vitamin D. Before the draw, show the label, the daily dose and when you last took it.',
            'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Before a blood draw most people remember their prescription medicines. Few people also pull a bottle for hair or nails from their bag. Yet that bottle may contain a high dose of biotin, vitamin B7. With some laboratory methods this vitamin can push a result up or down. The number on the printout may then describe the supplement, not the bone or the gland. (<a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/drugs-health-products/medical-devices/biotin-risk-false-lab-test-results.html">Health Canada, 2025</a>)</p>
<p>For nephrology patients the tests that matter most include parathyroid hormone (PTH), which helps regulate calcium and phosphate, and 25-OH vitamin D, used to judge vitamin D stores. With some sensitive methods biotin can lower the measured PTH and raise the vitamin D result. The clinician may then receive a misleading picture of bone and mineral metabolism. (<a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7467599/">Plasse et al., Clinical Kidney Journal</a>)</p>
<p>A published case report describes this problem in a patient on haemodialysis taking 10 mg of biotin daily. After the supplement was stopped, the results moved markedly back toward the earlier values. It is a documented single case that shows a possible risk. It cannot tell us how often similar errors occur in dialysis patients. (<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32905171/">PMID 32905171</a>)</p>
<p>AACC guidance recommends that the clinician and the laboratory work together when such interference is suspected. When kidney function is reduced, biotin clearance can take longer. The interval between the last dose and the blood draw must therefore reflect the dose, kidney function and the assay in use. The laboratory may also suggest confirming the result with another test. (<a href="https://myadlm.org/science-and-research/academy-guidance/biotin-interference-in-laboratory-tests">AACC Guidance Document, 2020</a>)</p>
<p>Before a planned draw, bring the pack or a photograph of the supplement, the daily dose and the time of the last intake. Tell the clinical team about biotin even if you stopped it recently. Any pause, and how long it should last, is agreed with the doctor and the laboratory. A result that guides bone treatment deserves to know whether a shelf supplement also entered the sample.</p>
<p><em>This article is for information only and does not replace individual medical advice. Whether to pause a supplement before a blood draw is decided by your treating physician together with the laboratory.</em></p>
<p><small><em>Sources verified 27 November 2026: <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32905171/">Plasse RA, Olson SW, Yuan CM, Nee R. Clinical Kidney Journal</a>; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1093/jalm/jfz010">AACC Guidance Document on Biotin Interference in Laboratory Tests, 2020</a>; <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/drugs-health-products/medical-devices/biotin-risk-false-lab-test-results.html">Health Canada. Biotin and the risk of false lab test results, 2025</a>.</em></small></p>

